   "l": "The Way bears one,\none bears two,\ntwo bears three,\nthree bears the ten-thousand things.\nThe ten-thousand things carry the shaded on their backs and hold the sunlit in their arms,\nand with the welling breath they make their blending.\nWhat people loathe\nis just 'orphaned', 'scant', 'ungood',\nand yet kings and dukes take these as their styles.\nSo with things: take from them and they gain,\nadd to them and they lose.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The Way brings forth one; one brings forth two; two, three; and three the ten thousand things. The ten thousand things bear yin at their backs and hold yang in their arms, and the breath that wells between them makes their accord. What people most hate is to be called orphaned, scant, ungood — and these are the very names kings and dukes take for themselves. So it is with things: take from them and they gain by it, add to them and they lose.",
   "n": "The counting lines are the most quoted cosmogony in the book and the text does not say what one, two and three are. Wang Bi answers with an argument about words rather than about stuff: the ten thousand things all come back to one; the one is reached by way of what is not; but once you have said 'one' you have a word as well as a thing, and a word plus a thing is already two; and having one and two you go on to three. On that reading the series is not a description of matter but a demonstration that counting, once begun, runs away with you — which is why it stops at three and jumps straight to 'the ten thousand'.\n\nThe other old reading takes the numbers as things, and the line that follows encourages it: one is the undivided breath, two are yin and yang, three is what they make between them. Both readings are ancient. Nothing in the four lines decides between them.\n\n陰 and 陽 begin as topography — the shaded and the sunlit face of a hill — and the literal line keeps that, because the pair is doing physical work here: what is at your back and what is in your arms. English has since borrowed both words, and the idiomatic line uses them. This is their only appearance in the whole Daodejing, which surprises readers who expect the book to be built on them.\n\n沖 was met in chapter 4, 道沖而用之, where Wang Bi glossed it 虛 'void'. A hollow is also what a thing wells up out of, and both senses are wanted: the breath between the two aspects is empty, and it surges. 以 has lost its object again — 'by it' — exactly as in chapter 39's 得一以清.\n\n孤, 寡 and 不穀 were the real self-abasing styles of rulers, and chapter 39 has already shown them doing this work. The turn at the end is the chapter's point and the reason it was put together this way: subtraction that adds, addition that subtracts. Chapter 48 will state it as a method — 損之又損, take away and take away again.",
   "p": true
