   "l": "What people teach,\nI too teach.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "What others teach, I teach as well.",
   "n": "所 turns the verb after it into a noun — 所教 is 'what is taught' — and 之 before it ties that to 人. The same two particles built 人之所惡, 'what people loathe', a few lines back, and the echo is deliberate: the chapter is pairing what people hate with what people teach. 之 at the end has no stated antecedent: 'I teach it' (the same lesson) or 'I teach them' (the same people). We take the first; nothing in the Chinese chooses.\n\nThe line is abrupt and readers have long felt it so; a few editions try to mend the seam. Read against what precedes, the claim is that he is not bringing a rival doctrine. The commonplace — that the overbearing come to a bad end — is exactly what everybody already says, and he says it too. Wang Bi's comment turns on the manner rather than the content: his teaching does not force people into line but goes by what is so of itself."
