   "l": "The strong-beamed do not get their own death;\nI shall take this as the father of my teaching.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Men who throw their weight about do not die their own deaths; I take that for the father of my teaching.",
   "n": "強梁 is a compound: a roof-beam is the stiffest thing in a house, and a strong-beamed man is one who will not bend — brutal, overbearing. 強 keeps bad company throughout: 柔弱勝剛強 in chapter 36, 堅強者死之徒 in chapter 76, 攻堅強者 in chapter 78. Every compound it forms in this book is on the losing side of the argument, which runs the other way, toward 柔 and 弱.\n\n不得其死 is an idiom, and a chilling one: not 'they die' but 'they do not get the death that was theirs' — no bed, no age, no proper end. It survives in Chinese to this day.\n\n教父 is 'the father of the teaching', the maxim from which the rest is generated. It is one tap-unit here because the pair does what neither half does: 父 alone is a parent, and the phrase means a first principle. Note the choice this makes. He has just said he teaches what everyone teaches, and now he makes the commonplace the parent of the doctrine — the book grounding itself on the one thing nobody disputes."
